  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 236 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nBy Monday, the cold front is expected to stall somewhere across \nsouthern Missouri, ushering in a relatively mild and dry airmass \ninto most of the area. Confidence is still relatively high that this \nshould provide enough cooling to allow for all of our current heat \nheadlines to expire Sunday evening, although this may not be a slam-\ndunk in our southernmost Ozark counties depending on where exactly \nthe front stalls. There is still a reasonable potential for a \nweaker/slower front to stall before it completely exits the area, \nand if this materializes, places like Reynolds/Iron/Madison counties \nin southeast Missouri may hover near Advisory levels of heat (100-\n105 degree heat index) Monday and Tuesday, before heat returns back \nnorth slightly ahead of another cold front Wednesday. In any case, \nMonday and Tuesday should, for the most part, feature more \ncomfortable temperatures/humidity, and low chances (~20%) for \nadditional showers in the vicinity of the stalled front. \n\nOn Wednesday, confidence continues to increase that a deeper, \nstronger shortwave will dig into the Great Lakes, further suppress \nthe central CONUS upper ridge, and send a reinforcing cold front \ninto the area sometime during the day. There remains a high degree \nof timing variability among members, with LREF cluster analysis \nunsurprisingly suggesting that a weaker shortwave would lead to a \nslower/weaker front and warmer Wednesday temperatures. This is also \nevident in much wider temperature spreads Wednesday, as some areas \nmay actually see another day of Advisory-level heat and humidity \nWednesday, while other areas stay relatively mild. The distribution \nof such will all depend on how quickly this front passes through our \narea. Meanwhile, confidence is also increasing that this front will \nbring another round of showers and thunderstorms between late \nTuesday and late Wednesday, with at least a modest potential for \nstrong or severe storms. Confidence remains low regarding this \npotential though, due to both the aforementioned timing uncertainty \nand limited wind shear. Behind the cold front though, trends \ncontinue to drift towards further temperature moderation, although \nwe do note that ensemble spreads remain relatively high, and \nlikewise forecast confidence in the current deterministic NBM \ntemperatures is relatively low.",
